This project evaluates whether generative data augmentation can improve the performance of a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) in classifying 75 different butterfly species. The researchers trained a Variational Autoencoder (VAE) and a Deep Convolutional Generative Adversarial Network (DCGAN) to synthesize new images, evaluating them using SSIM, FID, and Inception Score metrics. Despite the VAE achieving better overall generative quality, a strict CNN confidence filter (threshold of 0.85) was applied to the generated samples, resulting in only 120 accepted synthetic images heavily concentrated in just 5 classes. Consequently, retraining the baseline CNN on this augmented dataset decreased the weighted F1 score from 0.6135 to 0.5737, demonstrating that the generative models failed to provide the necessary balanced class diversity to improve model generalization.
